\section{Where the work is split}{

\code{\link[=rcausality]{rcausality()}} distributes the \emph{series pairs} over the workers when there
are at least as many pairs as workers, and otherwise the \emph{permutations or
surrogates} of every test (so that a two-series analysis on eight cores
still uses all eight). The two levels are never nested. The standalone
tests (e.g. \code{\link[=local_permutation_test]{local_permutation_test()}}, \code{\link[=transfer_entropy]{transfer_entropy()}}, \code{\link[=ccm]{ccm()}},
\code{\link[=hirata_rp]{hirata_rp()}}, \code{\link[=ccdh]{ccdh()}}, \code{\link[=marpid]{marpid()}}) parallelise their surrogate loop;
\code{\link[=bayes_granger]{bayes_granger()}} runs its chains in parallel; \code{\link[=benchmark_methods]{benchmark_methods()}}
distributes the simulated data sets. Parallel execution pays off when a
single unit of work takes more than a few milliseconds (kNN estimates,
recurrence counts, cross-map predictions, MCMC chains); for the cheapest
statistics the overhead of dispatching the work can exceed the gain.
}

\section{Reproducibility -- parallel results are \emph{identical} to serial ones}{

Every unit of work draws its random numbers from its own L'Ecuyer-CMRG
stream (\code{parallel::nextRNGStream()}), and the streams are derived from the
state of the session's generator at the time of the call. Consequently
\itemize{
\item the result of a call does not depend on \code{cores}: \code{cores = 1},
\code{cores = 2} and \code{cores = 8} give the same numbers (to the last digit)
for the same \code{seed}, on every platform, and
\item the generator of the session is left exactly where a serial call would
have left it, so a script that mixes Rcausality calls with other random
draws stays reproducible when \code{cores} changes.
Parallelisation therefore never changes a p-value, a Bayes factor or a
verdict; it only changes the wall-clock time. Use \code{seed} (or \code{set.seed()}
before the call) for reproducible runs as usual.
}
}

